---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/philosophy/foundational/what-is-philosophy">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Philosophy (Greek: <em>love of wisdom</em>) is the discipline of <strong>questions that
    every other field must assume answers to</strong>. A physicist assumes what counts as an
    explanation; a judge assumes what counts as fairness; you, reading this, assume you can
    know things and that the future will resemble the past. Philosophy is the discipline
    that stops assuming and asks: what <em>exists</em>? what can we <em>know</em>? what
    <em>should</em> we do? Its tools are not experiments or authorities but
    <strong>argument, definition and careful distinction</strong> — and its history is a
    2,500-year conversation in which the same questions recur with sharper instruments.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the discipline mapped: areas, method, and its habit of spawning sciences" widget="equationStep" config={whatCfg} />

  <p>
    The word "argument" needs reclaiming first. In philosophy an argument is not a quarrel
    but a structure: claims offered as support for another claim. This entire syllabus unit —
    "the language of argumentation" — is the discipline's core toolkit, and it is the most
    directly transferable content in the subject: learn to identify, test and dismantle
    arguments and you have learned the skill every field borrows from philosophy.
  </p>

  <h2>Identifying argument in text</h2>
  <p>
    The first skill is recognition. An argument hides in ordinary prose — an editorial, a
    lawyer's brief, a friend's text — and you find it by asking two questions: what is this
    passage trying to get me to accept (the <strong>conclusion</strong>), and what reasons
    does it offer (the <strong>premises</strong>)? Language gives markers for both:
    <em>therefore, so, thus, hence, it follows that</em> usually introduce conclusions;
    <em>because, since, given that, for the reason that</em> usually introduce premises.
    Try a worked example: "Since the vaccine trial showed 95% efficacy, and the side
    effects were mild, you should get vaccinated." Conclusion: get vaccinated. Premises:
    95% efficacy; mild side effects. Not every text argues — reports, descriptions and
    commands state or order, but do not infer — and the commonest reading failure is
    treating assertion as argument. Spotting <em>that</em> a reason was given, and what it
    was, is half of critical thinking.
  </p>

  <h2>The structure of argument: premises, conclusions, inference</h2>
  <p>
    Standardised, an argument is: premise 1, premise 2, … therefore conclusion — and the
    move from premises to conclusion is the <strong>inference</strong>, the only part that
    is strictly logical. The premises are where the substance lives: an argument is only as
    good as what you feed it, which is why philosophical training is double — check the
    inference (logic) and check the premises (the facts, the definitions, the assumptions
    smuggled in). A subtle skill is <strong>reconstruction</strong>: real arguments leave
    steps implicit ("she was in Paris at noon and in Rome at two — so she flew" quietly
    assumes travel time). Making hidden premises explicit is where most analysis happens,
    because hidden premises are where unsound arguments hide their work.
  </p>

  <h2>Three forms: deduction, induction, abduction</h2>
  <p>
    Arguments come in three canonical shapes. <strong>Deduction</strong> claims
    <em>necessity</em>: if the premises are true, the conclusion <em>must</em> be true —
    "All humans are mortal; Socrates is human; therefore Socrates is mortal." The
    conclusion cannot exceed the premises; it only makes explicit what they already
    contain. <strong>Induction</strong> claims <em>probability</em> from observed cases to
    unobserved ones: "Every raven I have seen is black, so all ravens are black." Science
    runs on it; it is never guaranteed (Europeans' swans were all white until black swans
    were seen in Australia) — the justification of induction itself is a famous
    philosophical problem (Hume's), taken up in the philosophy-of-science lesson.
    <strong>Abduction</strong> — inference to the best explanation — reasons from an
    observation to the hypothesis that would best account for it: "The grass is wet;
    sprinklers or rain; rain is forecast last night, so it rained." Diagnosis, detective
    work and theory choice all live here; abduction is how premises get generated in the
    first place. Each form has its own failure modes, which is why the three labels matter
    practically: demanding deductive certainty from an induction is a category error, and
    offering an induction where a deduction was promised is a cheat.
  </p>

  <h2>Validity and soundness</h2>
  <p>
    Two verdicts grade a deductive argument, and confusing them is the classic beginner
    error. <strong>Validity</strong> is about form: an argument is valid if the conclusion
    follows from the premises — <em>if they were true, the conclusion could not be false</em>,
    regardless of whether they actually are. <strong>Soundness</strong> is validity
    <em>plus</em> true premises. So: "All fish can fly; Nemo is a fish; therefore Nemo can
    fly" is valid and unsound — the logic is flawless; the premises are fiction. And "All
    cats are animals; some animals are black; therefore some cats are black" is
    <em>invalid</em> even though every statement in it is true — the form licenses the move
    no more than a broken bridge licenses crossing. The grading matters beyond philosophy
    exams: when a real argument fails, you want to know <em>where</em> — bad logic (fix
    the form) or bad premises (fix the facts) — and validity/soundness is precisely that
    diagnostic.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "if the conclusion is true, the argument was good." A
    true conclusion can rest on invalid logic or false premises — the stop broken clock
    that reads right twice a day. The argument is the <em>route</em>, not the destination;
    philosophy grades the route.
  </p>

  <h2>Analogy, hypothetical reasoning and Ockham's Razor</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Arguments from analogy</strong> reason that because two things are alike in
    some respects, they are alike in the one at issue: "animals feel pain as we do; we
    wrong each other by causing pain; so we wrong animals." Their strength varies
    exactly with the <em>relevance</em> of the shared respects — pain-relevant
    similarities (nervous systems) carry the argument; surface ones (both are furry) do
    not — and the standard test is to look for a <strong>disanalogy</strong> that breaks
    the bridge. <strong>Hypothetical reasoning</strong> works with conditional
    (<em>if…then</em>) claims: from "if P then Q" plus P, infer Q (affirming the
    antecedent — valid); from "if P then Q" plus not-Q, infer not-P (modus tollens —
    valid, the form behind every experimental falsification); but "if P then Q" plus Q
    does <em>not</em> give P (affirming the consequent — the formal name for the
    detective's classic error). <strong>Ockham's Razor</strong> — entities should not be
    multiplied beyond necessity — is the abductive tie-breaker: between two explanations
    accounting equally well for the data, prefer the one with fewer assumptions. It is a
    methodological preference, not a truth-guarantee (sometimes the extra entity is
    real), and its honest use is what separates theory choice from prejudice.
  </p>

  <h2>Flaws in argument: the fallacies</h2>
  <p>
    Named fallacies are diagnostic labels for recurring failure patterns.
    <strong>Circularity</strong> (begging the question): the conclusion is assumed among
    the premises — "the Bible is true because it says so and it is the word of God"
    assumes what it claims to prove; the argument goes nowhere even if the conclusion
    happens to be true. <strong>Contradiction</strong> in premises is fatal to
    soundness, since anything follows from a contradiction (explosion) — so exposing an
    inconsistency among an opponent's commitments remains philosophy's sharpest move.
    The classic company: <em>ad hominem</em> (attacking the arguer, not the argument),
    <em>straw man</em> (refuting a distorted version), <em>false dilemma</em> (pretending
    only two options exist), <em>hasty generalisation</em> (induction from too few
    cases), <em>post hoc ergo propter hoc</em> (sequence mistaken for causation),
    <em>appeal to authority</em> (authority cited outside its competence), <em>equivocation</em>
    (a key word shifting meaning mid-argument — "a feather is light; what is light cannot
    be dark; therefore a feather cannot be dark"). Two caveats keep the list honest:
    labelling is not refuting (you must still show <em>why</em> the move fails), and the
    fallacy fallacy cuts both ways — a fallacious argument for a claim does not falsify
    the claim; it merely fails to establish it.
  </p>

  <h2>The vocabulary: analytic/synthetic, a priori/a posteriori, necessary/contingent</h2>
  <p>
    Philosophy's terminological distinctions work like precision screwdrivers.
    <strong>A priori vs a posteriori</strong> is about how a claim can be <em>known</em>:
    a priori — by reasoning alone, without observation (mathematics, logic: "7 + 5 = 12");
    a posteriori — by experience ("copper conducts electricity"). <strong>Analytic vs
    synthetic</strong> is about what a claim <em>contains</em>: analytic — true in virtue
    of meanings ("all bachelors are unmarried"); synthetic — true in virtue of how the
    world is ("bachelors tend to die younger"). <strong>Necessary vs contingent</strong>
    is about modal status: necessary truths could not have been false (2 + 2 = 4, in
    any possible world); contingent truths happen to hold but might not have (the
    Eiffel Tower is in Paris). Standard pairings hold — a priori truths tend to be
    analytic and necessary; a posteriori ones synthetic and contingent — and the
    exceptions are famous: Kant's claim that some truths are synthetic a priori
    ("every event has a cause"), and Kripke's argued necessity of a posteriori truths
    ("water is H₂O"). The distinctions matter because much philosophical progress is
    discovering <em>which kind of question</em> one is asking — and much confusion
    dissolves when the kinds are separated.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The five core areas (widget above) map onto the rest of this subject's ladder:
    <strong>metaphysics</strong> (what exists), <strong>epistemology</strong> (what we can
    know), <strong>ethics</strong> (what we should do), <strong>logic</strong> (what follows
    from what — the toolkit you have just learned, formalised), <strong>aesthetics</strong>
    (what beauty is), with the applied philosophies of mind, science, law and language as
    these five pointed at a domain. The history is best read as the conversation itself:
    Socrates' questioning and Plato's forms; Aristotle's logic and ethics; the medieval
    synthesis of faith and reason; Descartes' founding of modern philosophy on radical
    doubt; Hume's scepticism about causation and induction; Kant's synthesis of the two;
    the analytic turn to language in the 20th century, alongside phenomenology on the
    continent — each generation sharpening the instruments it inherited.
  </p>
  <p>
    The honest case for studying it is threefold. It <strong>trains precision</strong>:
    philosophy students score at the top on verbal reasoning and logic tests (GRE data) —
    the transferable skill is spotting bad arguments, including your own. It
    <strong>clarifies the foundations of every other subject</strong>: what counts as
    evidence (science), fairness (law), consciousness (neuroscience) — each field hits
    these walls eventually. And it <strong>keeps the not-yet-answerable questions
    alive</strong>: philosophy is where questions go to become sciences — physics was
    "natural philosophy", psychology, economics and linguistics each left the fold when
    their methods grew precise enough — and the questions not yet ready to graduate (AI
    ethics shaping regulation, philosophy of science reshaping the replication-crisis
    response) are precisely where the subject is most useful today.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Nagel, What Does It All Mean?; Russell, The Problems of Philosophy; Copi &amp; Cohen, Introduction to Logic; Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy (plato.stanford.edu).</p>
</TopicPage>
